    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Romantic- and Revolution-era French naturalist Jean-Baptiste Lamarck is an\r\nimportant precursor for Jablonka’s and Ginsburg’s theory of living beings as beings\r\nthat learn. Lamarck defined living beings as beings that compose and create. Like\r\nJablonka and Ginsburg’s learning theory, Lamarck’s composing and creating theory\r\nlocates life in the capacity for a kind of purposeful striving. A consideration of his\r\ntheory can suggest fundamental questions for Jablonka and Ginsburg regarding the\r\nrelations among what they call “vivaciousness,” the state of living organisms, learning,\r\nand evolutionary transformation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
